\section{The leading term and its normalization}\label{sec:prefactor}

The marked ratios determine a differential equation for the leading
term, but leave a multiplicative constant. The equation comes from the
dilaton identity \eqref{eq:TL12}, which expresses the \(Y\)-derivative of
\(\log\Phi_g^{(p)}\) through the degree profiles \(c_{g,p+1}(d,0^p)\); to
obtain it with error \(O(h^{-2})\) we need the \(1/h\) term of these
profiles. We first compute this degree correction, then integrate it and determine the
constant from pure $\psi$ intersections. The resulting estimate is
Proposition~\ref{prop:prefactor}; its fixed-cusp specialization proves
Corollary~\ref{cor:constant}. Matching at a point that approaches the
critical endpoint polynomially in the genus keeps the integrated error
uniform on the whole interval.

Write $\bar K=\bar K_Y$ and $\bar H=\bar H_Y$, and introduce the kernel moments
\begin{equation}
 \Theta=\sum_{\ell\ge0}w_\ell=-\frac1{\bar K'(1)},\qquad
 M_1=\sum_{\ell\ge0}\ell w_\ell=\frac{\bar K''(1)}{2\bar K'(1)^2},\qquad
 \mathcal Z=Aar^{3/2}=\sqrt2 I.
 \label{eq:PF1}
\end{equation}
Primes on $\bar K,\bar H$ mean differentiation in their argument; derivatives of
parameter functions below are with respect to $Y$. All the moments in
\eqref{eq:PF1} are uniformly bounded on the closed normalized interval.
The variance $v_Y=\sigma_Y^2$ of the law in \eqref{eq:P12}, written $\sigma_Y^2$ in Section~\ref{the-positive-compact-family-of-kernels}, satisfies
\begin{equation}
 v_Y=\frac{15}{4}-\frac{\bar H''(1)}{\bar H(1)}=\frac1{2A\Theta}.
 \label{eq:PF2}
\end{equation}
Indeed $\bar H'(1)=-3\bar H(1)/2$ and
$\bar K'(1)=-5\bar H(1)/2+2\bar H''(1)/3$.

For fixed $p\ge1$, put $h=2g-2+p$ and define
\begin{equation}
 \mathcal J_{g,p}(Y)=
 \frac{\Gamma(h)4^{-g}}{\sqrt{\pi h}}
 \mathcal Z(Y)^{-h}r(Y)^{p/2}\sqrt{A(Y)\Theta(Y)}.
 \label{eq:PF3}
\end{equation}

