\section{The marked expansion beyond the leading term}\label{app:marked}

This appendix proves Theorems~\ref{thm:first-correction}
and~\ref{thm:full-expansion}. Subsections~\ref{app:second-degree}--\ref{app:critical-matching}
compute one further term in the recursion. The coefficient to be integrated
vanishes at the endpoint, so its primitive stays bounded. This gives both
an $O_p(g^{-1})$ relative error for the leading term and its first explicit
correction. The number $p$ of distinguished markings remains fixed.
Subsections~\ref{sec:full-expansion} and~\ref{sec:endpoint-expansion}
then construct the full series and control its remainder as the
truncation order grows.

\subsection{The second degree coefficient}\label{app:second-degree}

We first extend the degree expansion \eqref{eq:PF5} of the normalized
profiles by one order (Lemma~\ref{lem:second-profile}). The argument is
the adjacent-degree subtraction used for \eqref{eq:PF5} in
Section~\ref{sec:prefactor}, now
keeping the $h^{-2}$ terms of the prefactors and the two smallest
separating configurations.

We retain $\delta_p$ and $\beta_p$ from \eqref{eq:PF5} and
\eqref{eq:PF7}. The kernel coefficients $\theta_\ell$ are those of \eqref{eq:H2}. Set
\begin{equation}
 \alpha_p=A\{(p-3)\Theta+2M_1+2p-3\},\qquad
 \mathcal T_j=\sum_{\ell\ge\max(0,j)}w_\ell.
 \label{eq:ST1}
\end{equation}
For a pivot $i$, put $t=d_i$ and let $z$ count zero entries among the
other $p-1$ markings. Let $\mathbf d^{ij,\ell}$ be the merger child,
with new degree $t+d_j+\ell-1$, and let $\mathbf d^{i;u,v}$ be the
nonseparating child with the pivot replaced by $u,v$. Define
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
 B_{p,i}(\mathbf d)={}&
 A\sum_{j\ne i}(2d_j+1)
 \sum_{\ell\ge\max(0,1-t-d_j)}w_\ell
 [1-\beta_{p-1}-\delta_{p-1}(\mathbf d^{ij,\ell})]\\
 &+2A\sum_{\ell\ge\max(0,2-t)}w_\ell
 \sum_{\substack{u+v=t+\ell-2\\u,v\ge0}}
 [1-\beta_{p+1}-\alpha_p-\delta_{p+1}(\mathbf d^{i;u,v})]\\
 &+A^2\left\{\left[\binom z2+\frac{\theta_1}2\right]
                    \mathcal T_{2-t}+\frac12\mathcal T_{3-t}\right\}.
\end{split}
 \label{eq:ST2}
\end{equation}
The first line is absent at $p=1$. Starting at zero along a fixed
coordinate-increment path, define $e_p$ by
\begin{equation}
 e_p(\mathbf0)=0,\qquad
 e_p(\mathbf d+\mathbf e_i)-e_p(\mathbf d)=-B_{p,i}(\mathbf d).
 \label{eq:ST3}
\end{equation}
The formulas converge absolutely by \eqref{eq:TL10}; they give
$B_{p,i}=O_p((D+1)^3)$ and $e_p=O_p((D+1)^4)$.

ain $\mathsf R$ through order $t^K$
requires scalar coefficients only through order $K-r_*$.

The coefficients are computed as follows. Use the child tuples of
\eqref{eq:ST2}, and set $\mathcal W_{j,q}(\mathbf d)=4^j\overline
U_{j,q}(\mathbf d)$ for the exact bounded-complexity amplitudes.
At each order $K\ge1$, form
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
 \mathsf A_{p,i}={}&\frac{At}{1-t}\mathsf C_{p-1}\!\left(\frac t{1-t}\right)
 \sum_{j\ne i}(2d_j+1)
 \sum_{\ell\ge\max(0,1-d_i-d_j)}w_\ell
 \mathsf P_{p-1}\!\left(\mathbf d^{ij,\ell};\frac t{1-t}\right),\\
 \mathsf N_{p,i}={}&\frac{2At}{1-t}\mathsf C_{p+1}\!\left(\frac t{1-t}\right)
 \mathsf G_p(t)\sum_{\ell\ge\max(0,2-d_i)}w_\ell
 \sum_{u+v=d_i+\ell-2}\mathsf P_{p+1}\!\left(\mathbf d^{i;u,v};\frac t{1-t}\right).
\end{split}
 \label{eq:FE3}
\end{equation}
Nonnegative node degrees are understood, and $\mathsf A$ is absent
at $p=1$. The retained separating contribution is
\begin{equation}
 \mathsf S_{p,i}=
 \sum_{\substack{j\ge0,\ I\subset\{1,\ldots,p\}\setminus\{i\}\\
        s=|I|,\ (j,s+1)\text{ stable}\\2\le2j+s\le K}}
 \mathsf R_{p;j,s}(t)\sum_{\ell\ge0}w_\ell
 \sum_{u+v=d_i+\ell-2}
 \mathcal W_{j,s+1}(u,\mathbf d_I)
 \mathsf P_{p-s}\!\left(v,\mathbf d_J;\frac t{1-(2j+s)t}\right),
 \label{eq:FE4}
\end{equation}
where $J$ is the remaining set of labels. Only bounded $j$ occur;
for large $g$ these orient the smaller component uniquely. The two
orientations cancel the separating factor $1/2$. Define
\begin{equation}
 \begin{split}
 e_{p,K}(\mathbf0)&=0,\\
 e_{p,K}(\mathbf d+\mathbf e_i)-e_{p,K}(\mathbf d)
   &=-[t^K](\mathsf A_{p,i}+\mathsf N_{p,i}+\mathsf S_{p,i}),\\
 \mathsf C_p(t)&=\frac1A\sum_{d\ge1}b_d
       \mathsf P_{p+1}\!\left(d,0^p;\frac t{1+t}\right).
 \end{split}
 \label{eq:FE5}
\end{equation}
Finally, for $p\ge2$, KdV gives
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
 \mathsf G_p(t)={}&\mathsf P_p(\mathbf e_1;t)
 -3\sum_{\substack{0\le s\le p-2,\ j\ge0\\
             (j,s+2)\text{ stable}\\1\le2j+s\le K}}
  \binom{p-2}s\mathcal W_{j,s+2}(\mathbf0)\mathsf R_{p;j,s}(t),\\
 \mathsf G_p(h)&=\mathsf G_{p+1}(h+1)
                   \mathsf C_{p+2}(h)/\mathsf C_p(h).
\end{split}
 \label{eq:FE6}
\end{equation}
The last line descends from $p=2$ to $p=1$, and optionally to $p=0$.
It is an exact identity; its shift $h+1$ must be retained.

The coefficient and remainder bounds below are obtained by comparing
coefficientwise inequalities with an analytic system. We isolate the
elementary principle used for this comparison.

