\section{Coefficient extraction with inverse-genus errors}\label{app:extraction}

This appendix proves Theorems~\ref{thm:sharp-extraction}
and~\ref{thm:canonical-expansion}. Subsection~\ref{sec:quantitative-local}
makes the local limit theorem of Section~\ref{extracting-fixed-cusp-counts}
quantitative; its complex comparison and Fourier estimates are reused
afterwards. Subsections~\ref{app:local-complex}--\ref{app:small-indices}
prove Theorem~\ref{thm:sharp-extraction}, and
Subsections~\ref{app:phase-change}--\ref{app:canonical-proof} prove
Theorem~\ref{thm:canonical-expansion} and compute its first coefficient.

\subsection{A quantitative local limit}\label{sec:quantitative-local}

We first make the local limit theorem \eqref{eq:TL47} quantitative. The argument transfers a real-axis error to a complex neighborhood and applies Fourier inversion; the positive convolution factor controls the remaining part of the integral. This gives a power-rate bound. The complex comparison and Fourier estimates established in the proof will also be used in Subsection~\ref{app:local-complex}, where retaining a Poisson factor gives an inverse-genus error uniformly over all coefficient indices.

