\begin{proof}
The identity \eqref{eq:tight-insertion} follows from~\cite[Proposition~12]{BZ}. Indeed, their root and
moments are $R=Y/P$ and $M_j=P^jX^{(j+1)}(Y)$. With $q=P/2$,
their shifted times are $q^{k-1}t'_k$, where the $t'_k$ are the
times in \eqref{eq:H8}. The dimension constraint gives the factor
$q^{3g-3+p-|\mathbf d|}$. Combining their external insertion
$2L^{2d}/(4^d d!)$ with the free-energy factor $2^{3g-3}$ and
the double factorial in \eqref{eq:H6} gives
\eqref{eq:tight-insertion}. This also covers genus zero with $p\ge3$.

Equations~\eqref{eq:TL15} and~\eqref{eq:TL23} give \eqref{eq:tight-real}. For
the asymptotic estimate,
the factorial series with the extra weight
$|\mathbf d|(|\mathbf d|+1)$ is uniformly summable on the indicated
box. Thus the lengths may grow as $r(Y)^{-1/2}$.

For each fixed physical length vector, the proof of
\eqref{eq:TL33} applies to every amplitude in
\eqref{eq:tight-insertion}, which gives \eqref{eq:tight-positive}. The
finite sum in \eqref{eq:tight-insertion} and \eqref{eq:tight-real} justify
convergence throughout $0\le x<x_c$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Prescribing the cusp count}\label{subsec:tight-count}

We record the coefficient argument for the cut surfaces that occur in
the tight integration formula. Fix a number $k$ of cuts and put
$h_0=2g-2$. When $n\to\infty$, choose $x$ by
\[
 (h_0+1)\Psi(x)=n-1,\qquad
 \sigma^2=(h_0+1)\mathscr D\Psi(x),\qquad x=X(Y),
\]
and write $\alpha_x=2\varrho(Y)$. If $n$ stays bounded, we pass to a
subsequence on which $n$ is fixed and take instead $x=c/h_0$ with $c>0$.

